 ## Key differences
 
+### Resource ordering and dependencies
+
+Helm doesn't let a chart author declare that one resource depends on another. When Helm installs or upgrades a release, [the Helm docs](https://helm.sh/docs/topics/charts/#operational-aspects-of-using-dependencies) state that "the Kubernetes objects from the charts and all its dependencies are aggregated into a single set; then sorted by type followed by name; and then created/updated in that order." The order is a [hardcoded list of Kubernetes kinds](https://github.com/helm/helm/blob/main/pkg/release/v1/util/kind_sorter.go) in the Helm source, beginning with PriorityClasses and Namespaces, then Secrets and ConfigMaps, then CustomResourceDefinitions and RBAC, then Services and workloads, with any kind not on the list (including custom resources) last. Uninstall runs the same list in reverse. Helm sends the sorted manifests to the API server in a single pass and doesn't wait for one resource to become ready before applying the next.
+
+[Chart dependencies](https://helm.sh/docs/topics/charts/#chart-dependencies) don't change this. They're a packaging mechanism: [a subchart's objects are merged into the parent chart's set and sorted by kind](https://helm.sh/docs/topics/charts/#operational-aspects-of-using-dependencies) along with everything else, so a chart can't express "install the database subchart before the application subchart" or "start this Deployment after that Job completes." The workarounds are [chart hooks](https://helm.sh/docs/topics/charts_hooks/) ordered with `helm.sh/hook-weight` annotations, whose resources Helm doesn't manage as part of the release, or sequencing logic built into the application itself, such as init containers that poll for a dependency. Native sequencing for resources and subcharts has been proposed in [HIP-0025](https://helm.sh/community/hips/hip-0025/), which is still a draft.
+
+Pulumi orders operations from the program's actual dependencies. Passing one resource's [output](/docs/iac/concepts/inputs-outputs/) into another, or setting the [`dependsOn`](/docs/iac/concepts/resources/options/dependson/) resource option, creates an edge in the dependency graph. The Kubernetes provider waits for each resource to become ready before creating the resources that depend on it, and deploys independent resources in parallel, so updates are both correctly ordered and faster than a strict serial sequence. Deletes follow the same graph in reverse. When you install a chart through Pulumi, [`helm.v4.Chart`](/registry/packages/kubernetes/api-docs/helm/v4/chart/) supports the `config.kubernetes.io/depends-on` annotation for ordering between objects inside the chart, and `dependsOn` on the chart for dependencies on resources outside it.
+
 ### Language support and the authoring experience
 
 Helm charts are YAML manifests interleaved with [Go templates](https://helm.sh/docs/chart_template_guide/), and dynamic behavior comes from template helpers and a `values.yaml` file. The model keeps charts portable across clusters but couples templating logic to text manipulation, which becomes harder to read as charts grow. Pulumi programs are written in general-purpose languages, so authors get typed inputs and outputs, loops, conditionals, classes, IDE features (autocomplete, refactoring, go-to-definition), and the testing frameworks that already exist in those ecosystems. Pulumi also supports [YAML](/docs/iac/languages-sdks/yaml/) for users who prefer a markup format.
 
 ### Scope of resources managed
 
-Helm manages Kubernetes resources only. A chart can install Custom Resource Definitions, workloads, services, and other manifests into a cluster, but anything outside the cluster — a managed database, a DNS record, an IAM role, a managed Redis instance — needs another tool. Pulumi manages those resources and Kubernetes resources in one program through the [Pulumi Registry](/registry/), which covers AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, Kubernetes, and SaaS platforms like Datadog, Auth0, GitHub, and Cloudflare.
+Helm manages Kubernetes resources only. A chart can install Custom Resource Definitions, workloads, services, and other manifests into a cluster, but anything outside the cluster, such as a managed database, a DNS record, an IAM role, or a managed Redis instance, needs another tool. Pulumi manages those resources and Kubernetes resources in one program through the [Pulumi Registry](/registry/), which covers AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, Kubernetes, and SaaS platforms like Datadog, Auth0, GitHub, and Cloudflare.
 
 ### Execution and rollbacks
 
-Helm runs locally as the `helm` CLI against a Kubernetes cluster, recording each install or upgrade as a numbered release revision stored in the cluster. [`helm rollback`](https://helm.sh/docs/helm/helm_rollback/) restores a release to a prior revision from that history. Pulumi runs through the local CLI, programmatically through the [Automation API](/docs/iac/concepts/automation-api/), or remotely through [Pulumi Deployments](/docs/deployments/). Failed Pulumi updates leave the stack in a partially-updated state; you roll forward by reverting program code and running `pulumi up`. The two models trade off differently between fast revision-based rollback (Helm) and direct, scriptable control across resources from many providers in one update (Pulumi).
